⟦1200⟧{{smaller block|It seems likely that measurements of vapour pressure and compressibility may eventually enable us to determine accurately osmotic pressures in cases where direct measurement is impossible.}}

⟦1300⟧The slope of the temperature vapour pressure curves in the neighbourhood of the freezing point of the solvent is given by
{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|Freezing Point.—Solutions.}}
the latest heat equation. The difference in the two slopes for water and ice is <math>dp/d\text{T}-dp'/d\ \text{T}=\text{L}/\text{T}v</math>, where <math>\text{L}</math>, the latent heat of fusion, is the difference between the heats of evaporation for ice and water, and <math>v</math> is the specific volume of the vapour.

⟦1600⟧{{smaller block|The difference in the lowering of vapour pressures <math>dp-dp'</math> may be put equal to <math>\text{V}d\text{P}/v</math>, where <math>\text{P}</math> is the osmotic pressure, and <math>\text{V}</math> the specific volume of the solvent. We then get <math>\text{V}d\text{P}=\text{L}d\text{T}/\text{T}</math>.}}

⟦1700⟧In order to integrate this expression we need to know <math>\text{L}</math> and <math>v</math> as functions of the temperature and pressure. The latent heat <math>\text{L}</math> at any temperature is given by <math>\text{L}=\text{L}_0-\int_{\theta_0}^{\theta}(s-s')d\text{T}</math>, where <math>\text{L}_0</math> is value at <math>\text{T}_0</math> and <math>s-s'</math> is the difference in the specific heats of water and ice. The probable error in neglecting any variation of specific heat is small, and we may calculate <math>\text{L}</math> from the values of <math>\text{L}_0-(s-s')\ (\text{T}_0-\text{T})</math>, where <math>s-s'</math> is about 0·5 calories. The variation of <math>\text{L}</math> with pressure is probably small.

⟦2000⟧{{smaller block|The volume of a gramme of water also depends on temperature and pressure. Approximately one degree lowering of freezing point corresponds with a change of 12 atmospheres in the osmotic pressure. From the known coefficients of compressibility and thermal expansion we find that <math>\text{V}</math> may be represented by the linear equation <math>\text{V}=1{\cdot}000+0{\cdot}0008\ \Delta</math>, where <math>\Delta</math> is the lowering of the freezing point below 0°.}}

⟦2200⟧{{smaller block|Putting in these values and integrating we have, neglecting terms involving <math>\Delta^3</math>, <math>\text{P}=12{\cdot}06\ \Delta-0{\cdot}021\ \Delta^2</math> where <math>\text{P}</math> is the osmotic pressure in atmospheres.}}

